INFO 02-21 09:29:06 logger.py:39] Received request cmpl-0218042ba7a8423a86253852a30ec011-0: prompt: 'To flatten an array without using the `flat` method, you can use a combination of the `Array.prototype.concat` and `Array.prototype.reduce` methods. This will allow you to flatten an array to any depth, even in older versions of JavaScript that do not support the `flat` method.\n\nHere is an example of how you can use the `concat` and `reduce` methods to flatten an array:\n```\nconst arr = [1, [2, 3], [4, [5, 6]]];\n\nconst flatArr = arr.reduce((acc, val) => {\n  // If the current value is an array, concatenate it with the accumulator\n  // Otherwise, push the current value onto the accumulator\n  return Array.isArray(val) ? acc.concat(val) : acc.concat([val]);\n}, []);\n\nconsole.log(flatArr); // [1, 2, 3, 4, [5, 6]]\n```\nIn this example, the `reduce` method is used to iterate over the elements of the `arr` array. For each element, the `reduce` callback checks if it is an array, and if it is, it concatenates the element with the accumulator. If the element is not an array, it is pushed onto the accumulator. This process continues until all elements of the `arr` array have been processed, resulting in a flattened array.\n\nTo flatten the array to a deeper depth, you can call the `reduce` method recursively on the sub-arrays. Here is an example of how you can do this:\n```\nconst arr = [1, [2, 3], [4, [5, 6]]];\n\nconst flatArr = arr.reduce((acc, val) => {\n  // If the current value is an array, recursively flatten it and concatenate it with the accumulator\n  // Otherwise, push the current value onto the accumulator\n  return Array.isArray(val) ? acc.concat(flatArr(val)) : acc.concat([val]);\n}, []);\n\nconsole.log(flatArr); // [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]\n```\nIn this example, the `reduce` method is called recursively on any sub-arrays that are encountered. This will continue until all sub-arrays have been flattened, resulting in a completely flattened array.\n\nYou can use this approach to flatten an array to any depth, even in older versions of JavaScript that do not support the `flat` method. For more information and examples, please refer to the official JavaScript documentation.', params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.0, temperature=0.0, top_p=1.0, top_k=-1, min_p=0.0, seed=None, stop=[], stop_token_ids=[], bad_words=[], include_stop_str_in_output=False, ignore_eos=False, max_tokens=4, min_tokens=0, logprobs=None, prompt_logprobs=None, skip_special_tokens=True, spaces_between_special_tokens=True, truncate_prompt_tokens=None, guided_decoding=None), prompt_token_ids: [128000, 1271, 46443, 459, 1358, 2085, 1701, 279, 1595, 27323, 63, 1749, 11, 499, 649, 1005, 264, 10824, 315, 279, 1595, 1895, 6131, 15614, 63, 323, 1595, 1895, 6131, 24726, 63, 5528, 13, 1115, 690, 2187, 499, 311, 46443, 459, 1358, 311, 904, 8149, 11, 1524, 304, 9191, 11028, 315, 13210, 430, 656, 539, 1862, 279, 1595, 27323, 63, 1749, 382, 8586, 374, 459, 3187, 315, 1268, 499, 649, 1005, 279, 1595, 20773, 63, 323, 1595, 27369, 63, 5528, 311, 46443, 459, 1358, 512, 14196, 4077, 1040, 2961, 284, 510, 16, 11, 510, 17, 11, 220, 18, 1145, 510, 19, 11, 510, 20, 11, 220, 21, 5163, 4926, 1040, 10269, 9014, 284, 2961, 24726, 1209, 4575, 11, 1062, 8, 591, 341, 220, 443, 1442, 279, 1510, 907, 374, 459, 1358, 11, 78884, 433, 449, 279, 57412, 198, 220, 443, 18715, 11, 4585, 279, 1510, 907, 8800, 279, 57412, 198, 220, 471, 2982, 32909, 9276, 8, 949, 1046, 15614, 9276, 8, 551, 1046, 15614, 2625, 838, 2622, 2186, 42501, 5467, 1699, 968, 5641, 9014, 1237, 443, 510, 16, 11, 220, 17, 11, 220, 18, 11, 220, 19, 11, 510, 20, 11, 220, 21, 14623, 14196, 4077, 644, 420, 3187, 11, 279, 1595, 27369, 63, 1749, 374, 1511, 311, 30729, 927, 279, 5540, 315, 279, 1595, 1138, 63, 1358, 13, 1789, 1855, 2449, 11, 279, 1595, 27369, 63, 4927, 12621, 422, 433, 374, 459, 1358, 11, 323, 422, 433, 374, 11, 433, 41072, 988, 279, 2449, 449, 279, 57412, 13,